# Svelte 5 Conventions

- Use runes, never legacy syntax: `let x = $state(0)`, `const y = $derived(x * 2)`, `$effect(() => {...})`.
- Props: `let { title, onSave }: Props = $props();` with a typed `interface Props`. Mark two-way props with `$bindable()`.
- Events: use DOM attribute syntax `onclick={handler}`, never `on:click`. Component callbacks are plain props (`onSave`), never `createEventDispatcher`.
- Snippets instead of slots: `{#snippet children(item)}...{/snippet}` + `{@render children(item)}`.
- Always key each blocks: `{#each items as item (item.id)}`.
- Runes work in `.svelte.ts` modules — put shared reactive logic there instead of stores. Stores only for truly global state.
- Never mutate props directly; derive locally or use callbacks.

## Component structure order

1. `<script lang="ts">` — types, runes, helpers
2. Markup
3. `<style>` — scoped, only when utilities cannot express it

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  interface Props {
    label: string;
    count?: number;
    onSave?: (n: number) => void;
  }

  let { label, count = 0, onSave }: Props = $props();

  let doubled = $derived(count * 2);
</script>

<button onclick={() => onSave?.(doubled)}>{label}: {doubled}</button>
```
